    axes: { harm: 1, pitch: 1, ornament: 0, meter: 1, density: 1, transmission: 1, improv: 0, soundTech: 1, intensity: 0, voice: 2, timbre: 1, percussion: 1, cyclicity: 1 },
    description: 'Reggae fusion modern (codified 1990s onward through Jamaican-and-international artists blending reggae with hip-hop, R&B, and pop production aesthetics, with continuous tradition through to present), the contemporary genre-crossing reggae production. Defined by reggae rhythmic foundation (one-drop or rockers-style drum patterns, off-beat skanking guitar or keyboards, deep bass) overlaid with dancehall-derived programmed elements, hip-hop-and-R&B production techniques, and pop-song-form structural conventions, vocal performance combining reggae-singer or dancehall-deejay delivery with R&B-melismatic-and-pop-melodic vocal techniques, lyric content addressing romantic-and-social-commentary subjects in English-and-Patois code-switching, and chart-oriented commercial production polish targeting cross-genre playlists. Distinguished from straight roots-reggae by post-1990 timestamp and pop-and-R&B-production blending; distinguished from straight dancehall by retention of reggae melodic-and-instrumental-tradition over digital-riddim foreground; distinguished from straight pop by retention of reggae rhythmic-signature foundation.',
    exemplars: ['Sean Paul', 'Damian Marley', 'Shaggy'],
