    description: 'Welsh hymn balladry / cerdd Cymreig (codified 18th-19th century onward in Welsh-Methodist-revival-era congregational singing, with continuous tradition through to present and broader Welsh-language folk-revival from 20th century onward), the Welsh-language religious-and-secular ballad-singing tradition. Defined by Welsh-language vocal performance (the deliberate Welsh-language commitment as cultural-identity preservation), Methodist-revival hymn-singing tradition heritage with characteristic four-part SATB hymn-singing in Welsh-translated and original-Welsh-composed hymns, narrative-balladic repertoire spanning religious-Methodist hymn material, secular-folk ballads addressing Welsh-cultural-and-historical subjects, harp accompaniment in some traditional contexts (the triple-harp Welsh-traditional instrument), and performance contexts spanning chapel-congregational singing, eisteddfod (Welsh-cultural-festival) competition, and family-and-community gatherings. Distinguished from English hymnody by Welsh-language commitment and Methodist-revival-Welsh-specific repertoire; distinguished from broader Celtic balladry by Welsh-language and harp-tradition; distinguished from later Welsh choral tradition by retention of solo-and-small-ensemble balladic format alongside congregational-choral material.',
