    description: 'Piedmont fingerpicking blues (codified 1920s-1940s onward across the Piedmont region of Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina, and Georgia, with second-wave 1960s-1970s folk-revival adoption establishing the concert-performance repertoire), the eastern-seaboard fingerpicked-blues tradition. Defined by alternating-thumb fingerpicking-guitar foundation (the Piedmont signature where thumb plays steady-bass-alternation pattern while fingers play melodic-and-chord-counterpoint above), narrative-and-songster repertoire combining blues compositions with broader Anglo-Celtic ballad and dance repertoire, ragtime-derived harmonic vocabulary with extended-chord voicings (the Piedmont specialty drawn from late-19th-century parlor and ragtime guitar repertoire), Appalachian-tradition crossover with white old-time fingerpicking traditions, and intimate vocal-and-guitar duo or solo-performer format. Distinguished from Delta blues by alternating-thumb fingerpicking foundation and ragtime-derived harmonic vocabulary over slide-guitar-and-modal-foundation; distinguished from Texas blues by eastern-not-Texas regional lineage; distinguished from country fingerpicking by African-American-tradition-foreground over white-Appalachian.',
