    description: 'The Brazilian Portuguese cantiga de ninar (also cantiga de embalar, acalanto brasileiro) cradle-song tradition; defined by fusion of Iberian Portuguese acalanto inheritance with Indigenous-Brazilian melodic-vocabulary contributions and Afro-Brazilian rhythmic inflection, modal-major melodic primary-affect with characteristic Brazilian-popular-music sevenths-and-ninths chord vocabulary when guitar-accompanied, ⁶⁄₈ gentle rocking, frequent presence of accompanying nylon-string-guitar in semi-domestic-recording contexts, Brazilian-Portuguese literary-text register including regional folksong survival. The Heitor Villa-Lobos "Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5" Aria first movement is the canonical art-music elevation of the cradle-song impulse in Brazilian classical-music.',
    exemplars: ['"Boi da cara preta" (traditional)', '"Nana neném" (traditional)', 'Heitor Villa-Lobos — Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 Aria (cradle-song-derived 1938)', 'Vinícius de Moraes / Toquinho — "Canto de Ossanha" cradle-adjacent', 'Egberto Gismonti — cradle-song recordings'],
