    description: 'The Arabic tahwidah (تهويدة, cradle-song) tradition continuous from medieval Arabic domestic-vocal practice across the Maghreb, Mashriq, and Gulf cultural zones into modern Arabic-language domestic singing; defined by Arabic-maqam modal-melodic-vocabulary (Bayāti, Saba, Hijaz, and other tender-affect maqamat common in tahwidah practice), microtonal quarter-tone pitch inflection, free-meter or slow rocking phrasing, melismatic ornament drawing on tarab vocal-expressive vocabulary, classical-Arabic or vernacular-dialect text register (Egyptian, Levantine, Maghrebi, Gulf regional variations), intimate maternal-singing performance context, occasional accompaniment by a soft duff frame-drum. Distinguished from Persian lālā\'ī by Arabic-maqam-not-Persian-dastgāh framework and by Arabic linguistic register.',
