    description: 'The Bengali kanna gaan (literally "weeping-song", also "vilāp gan" elegy-song) women\'s mourning-song tradition continuous from medieval-Bengal rural practice through colonial-period documented performance into modern Bangla-language domestic mourning practice across West Bengal and Bangladesh; less codified as a professional-role tradition than Tamil oppāri but documented in folk-ethnographic literature; Defined by free-meter melismatic Bangla-vocal phrasing, Bengali-folk-modal vocabulary related to but distinct from formal Hindustani-classical practice (Bhairavi and Bhupali frequent), sob-glottalization vocal technique, Bangla-language text register with frequent praise-of-the-deceased and grief-narrative lyric content, women\'s-voice primary tradition particularly performed by elder women of the family at the time of death.',
    exemplars: ['Bangla "vilāp gan" elegy-tradition (Tagore-era documented)', 'Rural West-Bengal kanna gaan (folk-ethnographic field-recordings)', 'East-Bengal Bangladesh-region funeral-lament repertoire', 'Lalon Fakir-tradition mourning-songs (Baul-adjacent)', 'Rabindranath Tagore — "Mritunjaya" art-song treatment of Bengali mourning'],
