    description: 'The Greek politicized éntechno ("artistic music") protest-song tradition centered on Mikis Theodorakis (1925-2021, the foundational figure whose Communist-Party-aligned political-songwriter career spanned the 1944-1945 Civil-War partisan era through the 1967-1974 Greek Junta exile-and-imprisonment era and continuous through post-Metapolitefsi democratic-era continuous performance — Theodorakis was arrested-imprisoned-and-internally-exiled during the Junta years 1967-1970 and his music was banned during that period); the canonical Theodorakis songbooks include the Mauthausen Trilogy 1965 (texts by Holocaust survivor Iakovos Kambanellis), Romiosini settings of Yannis Ritsos texts, and Epitaphios setting of Yannis Ritsos\'s 1936 elegy. Defined by bouzouki-and-guitar-and-voice songwriter-format with Greek-Eastern-modal melodic vocabulary, rebetiko-instrumental-lineage integration, classical-Greek-poetic-text register (Ritsos, Elytis, Seferis, Kambanellis poetic-text settings), political-anti-Junta-and-anti-fascist lyric content frequently encoded through poetic-allegory.',
