    description: 'The Arabic classical praise-poetry tradition continuous from pre-Islamic jāhiliyya-era qasida ode-form (the canonical pre-Islamic Mu\'allaqat seven-poem collection from 6th-7th century Arabian-peninsula) through medieval-Islamic-era continuous performance into modern Arabic-classical-poetry recitation practice; madh (praise) and qasida (extended-ode following the classical Arabic-poetry monorhyme-and-monometer form) are the canonical Arabic praise-poetry-and-song forms. Defined by oud-and-voice arrangement when sung (the qasida is foundationally a recited-poetic form rather than a sung-musical form, but sung-qasida traditions continue particularly in Andalusi-Maghreb and Levantine traditions), classical-Arabic text register with elaborate-rhetorical embellishment, melismatic vocal practice in sung-variants, praise of the patron-or-tribal-figure or in Islamic-era praise-of-the-Prophet (the latter overlapping with the naat tradition).',
