    description: 'The Bulgarian harvest-song (zhetvarski pesni, "harvest-songs") tradition continuous from premodern Bulgarian-agricultural village-labor practice (the Bulgarian-village women-laborers performed harvest-songs throughout the summer harvest-season — grain-cutting, wheat-binding, threshing — with songs scheduled to specific labor-tasks) through 19th-century Bulgarian-folklorist documentation through 20th-century Le Mystère des Voix Bulgares-era canonization (the 1975 onwards Marcel Cellier-produced canonical recordings) into modern Bulgarian-folk-revival continuous practice. Defined by unaccompanied women\'s-group-vocal close-harmony arrangement, Bulgarian-language text register with characteristic harvest-folk vocabulary, characteristic close-second-interval Bulgarian-folk harmonic vocabulary, characteristic narrow-range terraced-melismatic-vocal practice.',
    exemplars: ['Le Mystère des Voix Bulgares — 1975-onward canonical', 'Trio Bulgarka — Yanka Rupkina, Stoyanka Boneva, Eva Georgieva canonical', 'Filip Kutev Ensemble — canonical Bulgarian-folk-choir', 'Pirin Folk Ensemble — canonical', '"Polegnala e Todora" canonical zhetvarska pesen'],
    crossRefs: ['fieldWork.eastAsian', 'wedding.european', 'lullaby.european'],
