    axes: { harm: -2, pitch: 0, ornament: 0, meter: 0, density: -2, transmission: -2, improv: 0, soundTech: -2, intensity: 0, voice: 2, timbre: 0, percussion: -2, cyclicity: 0 },
    description: 'The Japanese taue-uta (田植唄, "rice-planting songs") tradition continuous from premodern Japanese-agricultural village-labor practice through Edo-period continuous performance into modern Japanese-and-Japanese-diaspora continuous rural practice (where it survives — the documented decline of taue-uta practice is well-attested as Japanese-agricultural mechanization removed the labor-coordination need) and into intangible-heritage status for certain regional variants (the Mibu-no-Hana-Taue rice-planting-ritual of Hiroshima Prefecture). Defined by shamisen-and-voice arrangement (and occasionally fue-flute and small-drum accompaniment in some regional variants), Japanese-language text register, Japanese-in-scale or yo-scale pentatonic vocabulary, characteristic women-singers (sa-otome rice-planting-women in some regional variants) labor-context.',
