    description: 'The Baaka (Aka, Bayaka) Central-African pygmy hunting-and-ritual polyphonic-vocal tradition continuous from premodern Congolese-and-Central-African-Republic rainforest hunting practice through 20th-century continuous performance (Simha Arom Polyphonies et polyrythmies instrumentales d\'Afrique centrale 1985 canonical scholarly source documents the complex-interlocking-hocketed-vocal structure of Baaka polyphony) into modern Baaka-and-broader-Mbuti continuous practice. The Baaka hunting-song polyphony is documented as among the most-complex unaccompanied-group-vocal polyphonic-traditions in the world; the Baaka use interlocking-hocket vocal-technique where individual singers contribute single-pitch-or-short-figure parts to a collective polyphonic-cycle, with characteristic yodel-and-melisma vocal-style. Defined by unaccompanied group-vocal hocket-and-yodel arrangement, Baaka-language text register, characteristic interlocking-polyphony group-vocal architecture, ritual-and-hunting performance-context.',
    exemplars: ['Simha Arom — Polyphonies et polyrythmies 1985 canonical scholarly', 'Baaka pygmy hunting-and-ritual song traditional field recordings', 'Centrafricaine Baaka polyphonic-vocal canonical', 'Bobangi-style call-and-response polyphony', 'Modern Aka-Baaka rainforest continuous-living tradition'],
