    description: 'The Inuit katajjaq (throat-singing) tradition continuous from premodern Inuit-Arctic-hunter-gatherer practice across Northern Canada (Nunavut, Nunavik, Nunatsiavut) and Greenland through colonial-era continuous performance (including a documented period of missionary-suppression in the late-19th-and-early-20th-century) into modern Inuit-cultural-revival; the katajjaq is performed as a vocal-game-and-competition between two women face-to-face, with characteristic interlocking-throat-sound arrangement evoking Arctic-environment sounds (wind, geese, dogs, walrus, mosquitoes). Defined by unaccompanied two-women-face-to-face hocket-and-throat-percussion arrangement, Inuktitut text register with characteristic vocables-and-ideophones, characteristic interlocking-percussive-vocal architecture.',
    exemplars: ['Tanya Tagaq — modern Inuit-throat-singing canonical', 'Madeleine Allakariallak — traditional Inuit katajjaq canonical', 'Sila — Inuit katajjaq women-duo canonical', 'PIQSIQ canonical Inuit-throat-singing modern recording', 'Modern Inuit katajjaq-revival continuous-living tradition'],
    crossRefs: ['huntingSong.centralAsian', 'huntingSong.indigenous', 'huntingSong.subSaharanAfrican'],
    status: 'documented',
