    description: 'The Irish-Gaelic spinning-and-domestic-labor song tradition continuous from medieval-and-early-modern Irish-rural domestic-textile-labor practice (the spinning-wheel-and-distaff context where women performed wool-and-flax spinning while singing) through 19th-20th century continuous performance into modern Irish-Gaelic-and-Scottish-Gaelic revival; foundational Irish spinning-song repertoire includes "Bean Phaidin" (Paddy\'s wife, Connemara canonical), "Eileanóir a Rún" (Eleanor my treasure), "An Mhaighdean Mhara" (The Mermaid, canonical Donegal-Gaelic), "Dónall Óg" (Young Donal). Defined by unaccompanied women\'s-solo or small-group vocal arrangement, Irish-Gaelic-language text register, characteristic Connacht-and-Donegal modal vocabulary, characteristic sean-nós-style melismatic-ornament practice.',
    exemplars: ['"Bean Phaidin" canonical Connacht Irish-Gaelic spinning-song', '"Dónall Óg" canonical Irish-Gaelic narrative-and-spinning', 'Mairéad Ní Mhaonaigh — modern Donegal-Gaelic canonical (Altan)', 'Iarla Ó Lionáird — modern sean-nós canonical', 'Connacht and Donegal sean-nós continuous-living tradition'],
