    description: 'The Yiddish spinning-and-domestic-lullaby tradition continuous from medieval-Ashkenazi-Jewish domestic-and-textile labor practice through 19th-century Yiddish folksongbook canonization (the canonical "Oyfn Pripetshik" 1880s by Mark Warshawsky is the foundational Yiddish learning-spinning-domestic song where the rabbi-teacher teaches the alef-beys alphabet to small-children with the women-mothers spinning by the hearth-fire — the song integrates Yiddish-domestic-textile-and-childcare context with Jewish-religious-learning) into modern Yiddish-revival continuous practice. Other foundational repertoire includes "Tumbalalaika" (riddle-song) and "Rozhinkes mit Mandlen" (Raisins and Almonds, the canonical Yiddish lullaby). Defined by unaccompanied women\'s-solo vocal arrangement, Yiddish-language text register, characteristic Ashkenazi-freygish modal vocabulary, characteristic hearth-and-spinning-wheel domestic-context.',
    exemplars: ['"Oyfn Pripetshik" 1880s Mark Warshawsky canonical Yiddish spinning-song', '"Rozhinkes mit Mandlen" canonical Yiddish lullaby-spinning crossover', 'Ruth Rubin — canonical 20th-century Yiddish-folk-vocal documentation', 'Theodore Bikel — canonical Yiddish-folk-revival 20th-century', 'Modern Yiddish-revival continuous-tradition'],
    crossRefs: ['domesticRhythm.celticHebridean', 'lullaby.european', 'wedding.european'],
    status: 'documented',
