  "polish_death_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal.death","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":-1,"ornament":0,"meter":2,"density":2,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Fast, compact guitar attacks and bass reinforcement are punctuated by rapid kit figures and a harsh lead voice. The model selects speed and riff precision while retaining the band’s thrash ancestry; it is not a separate national tuning system. D-standard guitar, muted-chug technique and all recording parameters are authored.","exemplars":["Vader — Solitude in Madness"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "polish_folk_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal.folkMetal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"An electric guitar and bass foundation supports a prominent bowed cello line, voice and rock-kit percussion. The selected palette follows the credited Reakcja pogańska ensemble rather than importing the separate historical Percival project’s full instrument list. Cello phrases and vocal refrains answer weighty riffs in an authored folk-metal arrangement. Steel cello strings, modern cello setup, standard guitar tuning and the present-day chain are design choices, not a reconstruction of the 2009 record.","exemplars":["Percival Schuttenbach — Reakcja pogańska"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "polish_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Melodic electric-guitar leads and chordal riffs are supported by bass, stick-played drums and a sung lead voice. This authored heavy-metal branch uses standard-tuned guitar and a straightforward rock-band balance. It is a contemporary realization of documented ensemble roles, not a claim that every period of Turbo’s changing hard-rock/metal repertoire used one sound.","exemplars":["Turbo"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "polish_metalcore": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":-1,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":2,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Muted electric-guitar riffs, forceful bass and stop-start drum-kit accents frame the vocal lead. The arrangement selects tightly synchronized heavy sections and melodic releases from the documented metalcore/death/thrash overlap. It preserves the guitar, bass, drums and voice role set while using authored drop-C tuning and a cleanly separated contemporary recording chain; it does not reproduce the original sessions or apply later personnel to the 2004 recording.","exemplars":["Frontside — Zmierzch Bogów"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":["distortedRock.punk","distortedRock.metal.death"]},
