  "portuguese_black_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal.black","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":-1,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":2,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Tremolo guitar movement and melodic distortion surge over bass and rapid kit patterns beneath an intense voice, opening into quieter, spacious phrases. The selected song’s label account documents these contrasting roles. This is one Portuguese black-metal-rooted branch, not a statement that all of Gaerea’s later work remains strictly black metal. E-flat tuning, humbucker construction and modern room/chain are authored choices.","exemplars":["Gaerea — Unknown / Coma"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "portuguese_death_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal.death","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":-1,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":2,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Dense electric-guitar chugs and bass weight alternate with rapid kit attacks and short melodic lead turns beneath harsh voice. The band describes a deliberate expansion from direct brutality toward layered guitar solos, active bass and melodic structure. The selected core quartet leaves the album’s track-specific orchestration outside this realization. D-standard tuning and all hardware and production parameters are authored; they are not claims about the original record.","exemplars":["Analepsy — Quiescence"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "portuguese_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":-1,"ornament":1,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Dense melodic guitar and electric bass sustain a deliberate drum pulse while voice and layered keyboard atmosphere carry gothic drama. The model uses an unbranded sample-based workstation for the documented keyboard role, not a physical orchestra. Standard guitar tuning and the contemporary digital chain are authored. The selected band-core arrangement does not claim to include every guest string part or earlier experimental direction.","exemplars":["Moonspell — Far From God"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "post_black_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal.black","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":-1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Layered electric-guitar lines move between clear melodic space and saturated swells above bass and drum kit, with voice alternating prominence and blend. Neige’s interview describes Spiritual Instinct as a deliberately guitar/bass/drums/voice-focused record and explains moving beyond his original black-metal vocabulary. This model uses an authored humbucker guitar and digital studio chain; the source’s Jazzmaster and tape recording are not silently claimed as these defaults.","exemplars":["Alcest — Spiritual Instinct"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
