  "tanzanian_pop": {"parent":"functionalSong","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":-1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"The releasing label describes a Moshi pop trio formed in the early 1950s: Frank Humplick, Thecla Clara and Maria Regina, with harmonized singing and Frank guitar. The selected historical family-trio arrangement retains all three singers and guitar. This is separate from Frank later recording with the Jambo Boys band. Historical Tanzanian guitar-pop branch, distinct from contemporary Tanzanian hip hop and high-tempo singeli already cataloged. Three named vocalists are retained as a live group. Authored modern OM fingerpicking and clean digital environment do not claim the original 78rpm session construction or recording equipment. The label explicitly describes the complete trio, not an arbitrary reduction from Jambo Boys. Representative authored present-day recording arrangement for the selected documented branch. Sources establish category, roles and locality. Exact materials, makes, playing settings, tuning, room, signal chain and axes are authored choices, not measured source-session facts or universal genre characteristics.","exemplars":["Frank and His Sisters — family trio repertoire collected on Mississippi Records MRI-120 (2020 reissue)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
