  "musica_aragonesa": {"parent":"improvOnFrame","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":0,"voice":-2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"Aragón government performing-arts circuit publishes the management-supplied full75-minute instrumental duo program: Miguel Guallar on cello and violin, David Aznar on accordion. It explicitly identifies instrumental music and original compositions drawing on traditional dance rhythms, developed through improvisation and harmony. Creator-owned Música Aragonesa playlist includes Vegetal Jam Bosque encantado at item81. Crisol de Cuerda’s faculty biography independently confirms Aznar’s piano-key chromatic accordion and Zaragoza birth. The complete government-circuit stage program provides the source boundary. Three alternate instrument families across two musicians, no singer; metaphorical musical voices in the promotional text are not literal vocal credits. Cello and violin are Guallar’s alternate roles, not an invented simultaneous string section. The program is not the full studio albums, which can include further guest instruments and overdubs. The selected instrumental folk-dance branch is placed under improvOnFrame because the presenter explicitly describes improvisation over traditional rhythmic forms and harmonic development; this does not label all Aragón music improvisational or equate the region with jota alone. Authored legato bowed melody/response on modern cello and violin, dry steel-reed LMM accordion with bellows articulation, neutral present-day studio capture and equal temperament are concrete configuration choices; source makes, woods and exact articulation remain unmeasured. Distinct from Aragón jota, rock and pan-European bal-folk through the positive regional category and precisely scoped member program. Authored present-day recording arrangement based on the documented selected performance branch. Sources support category, credited roles and locality. Materials, makes, technique presets, tuning, studio, signal chain and axes are authored realizations, not measured historical session settings or universal genre rules.","exemplars":["Vegetal Jam — complete 75-minute instrumental duo program published in Aragón’s RAEE 2023 circuit catalogue"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "musica_ayacuchana": {"parent":"protestSong","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"Institutional exact track credits Manuel Silva alone on lead vocals and acoustic guitar. Smithsonian’s regional field guide identifies this interpretation with Ayacucho huayno and specifically describes nylon-string Spanish guitar accompanying sung text. Complete selected El Hombre recording, not the entire multi-artist huayno anthology. One singer, one guitarist, same person. No additional anthology instruments imported. The institutional field guide identifies the composition as a response to violence against protesting students and places the performance in Ayacucho’s testimonial song tradition; protestSong describes this selected branch, not every form of Ayacuchana music. Existing regional huayno, guitar and protest categories do not make the broader Ayacucho discovery category equivalent to any single one. Nylon-string guitar family is positively supported; cedar, rosewood, string tension, bracing, free-stroke fingerstyle realization and recording chain remain authored choices rather than source-session measurements. Vocal register, pitch organization, mechanism and culturally specific delivery remain source-unspecified. No unsupported creator-playlist membership is asserted. Authored present-day recording arrangement based on the documented selected performance branch. Sources support category, credited roles and locality. Materials, makes, technique presets, tuning, studio, signal chain and axes are authored realizations, not measured historical session settings or universal genre rules.","exemplars":["Manuel Silva — El Hombre, complete Huayno Music of Peru Vol.1 track21"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
