  "swazi_pop": {"parent":"functionalSong","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":0,"cyclicity":0},"description":"Artscape Theatre Centre announces Manana’s complete Romantic Tour solo acoustic concert in its Arena on23February2023, duration two hours, explicitly voice and piano. This is the announced whole solo format, not an inferred subset of his produced recordings or Seba Kaapstad lineup. Manana is the single performer providing both roles. The listing establishes the planned musical practice; no claim of personally observed performance or verified exact setlist. The separate BandaBanda organizer biography identifies his eSwatini birth, singer-songwriter/pianist work and jazz/choir training; it does not add guests from other recordings to this solo concert. Exact creator Swazi Pop playlist positively includes Manana at6,59,93, including a separately labeled acoustic recording at93; the selected2023 concert is an actual same-artist realization, not claimed to be that recording. The broader contemporary Eswatini popular-song discovery repertoire includes sung soul/pop artists alongside overlapping rap and electronic acts. Existing swazi_hip_hop explicitly models the regional MC/produced-rap compilation tradition; this positively sung solo songwriter branch is not exhausted by it. Existing south_african_pop documents Johannesburg bubblegum/kwaito-adjacent song practice; the Swazi-origin songwriter network has distinct regional scope while sharing a southern African performance circuit. Neither moving the concert to Cape Town nor using a different arrangement alone creates the category. Authored modern recording rendition of the documented solo voice-and-piano practice: coherent nine-foot Steinway D, block-chord pop accompaniment, neutral source-unspecified vocal mechanism/register/ornament, single accompanied singer. Piano make, material, pitch standard and touch are editorial choices, not claimed source equipment or transcribed harmony; single-lead production is authored, not measured take count. Treated room and clean digital chain are editorial, not an assertion about the Arena acoustics. Global tuning omitted; source trained-jazz biography does not establish current physiological technique. Broad functional-song placement describes the expressly authored song-harmonization branch, not universal Swazi music.","exemplars":["Manana — The Romantic Tour solo concert (2023)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "bloco": {"parent":"groovePercussion.brazilian","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":2,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":0,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Complete dated24-member Monobloco performing-group roster preserved by anthropologist Marina Bay Frydberg in2014 fieldwork paper, footnote12 explicitly attributed to contemporaneous artist website accessed1June2014. Five named singers: Biguli,Fábio Allman,Momo,Pedro Luís,Pedro Quental. Three cavaquinho players: Alessandro,Cachaça,Carlinhos. Momo also guitar; Igor Araújo chocalho/bass. André Schmidt and Leo Saad agogô; C.A.Ferrari repique; Caco Chagas,Gabriel Lopes,Mário Moura,Maurão surdo; Celso Alvim,Fred Castilho,Gui Rodrigues,Marcos Feijão,Pitito caixa; Fábio Groove,Júnior Teixeira,Sidon Silva tamborim. Ten family cards preserve all26 credited human-role assignments and24 people, not ten performers. This is the documented standing group, not the expanded Carnaval student parade or an inferred individual recording session. Five credited voices do not establish simultaneous harmony voicing. Bloco is the Brazilian street-carnival community and its year-round performing groups, represented by Rio Monobloco’s eclectic samba-based standing ensemble. Firsthand ethnographic source expressly distinguishes street-bloco history, professional shows and expanded parade participation from escola-de-samba competitions. Existing samba_enredo specifically narrates an annual school competition theme over massed bateria; existing bloco_afro is the historically distinct Salvador Afro-Brazilian black-consciousness/samba-reggae movement. These overlap broader bloco culture but neither covers this Rio eclectic street-bloco branch. No creator-map artist membership or universal instrumentation claim. Broad groovePercussion.brazilian follows the positively documented samba bateria-based eclectic dance practice. Cavaquinho samba and agogô bateria contexts are authored appropriate realization; no Portuguese fado or Candomblé context inferred. Source-generic guitar/bass map to authored electric single-coil guitar and electric bass; construction/gear are editorial, not documentary equipment. Caixas map to standalone snare, explicitly authored steel model not source hardware. Chocalho is source-neutral shaker, no inferred seed/jingle construction. One surdo family represents four players with authored primeira bass realization, not a claim all four play the same register. Repique uses authored existing samba repinique practice, not measured hand technique. All voice physiology and precise pitch practice remain source-unspecified; live-group voicing unknown. Controlled live-room acoustics are authored; unspecified capture hardware is omitted and no original session location is claimed. No global temperament asserted. Authored repair: a dual-single-coil fretted roundwound electric bass and controlled live-room acoustic replace the prior split-coil/neutral-studio realization. Unspecified capture apparatus is omitted. The five credited singers retain their count; small sung note groups per syllable are a newly authored musical choice, not measured source vocal technique or an extra clapping role. All other source roles remain intact.","exemplars":["Monobloco — complete24-member2014 documented standing group"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
