  "indie_sudcaliforniano": {"parent":"distortedRock.alternative","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":0,"cyclicity":0},"description":"The artist-issued album explicitly credits Jorge “Koky” Álvarez guitar and voice, José Antonio Cital bass and Webo drums, plus Omar de la Rosa requinto on Kinder. All four people and all five families are retained across the whole twelve-track album; the guest is not erased by choosing a convenient track. Manuel and Omar also produced/recorded/mixed, but those technical credits do not create further instrumental roles. Artist biography positively describes distorted guitars, energetic rhythms and rock/grunge in the Sudcalifornian alternative scene since the1990s. The exact creator programme positively includes Sónica at items4/57/92 alongside the broad Baja California Sur independent network, including guitar bands, hip-hop and experimental performers. Artist-owned primary independently locates this band and its alternative-scene practice in La Paz, Baja California Sur; this is more than an incidental recording visit. Full existing baja_indie concerns the northern Baja California/Tijuana network, not all of the peninsula. Existing musica_sudcaliforniana explicitly documents the regional traditional/corrido community, whose geographical overlap is acknowledged without treating its selected trio as its entire definition. Mexican_indie and indie_rock_mexicano remain broad national overlapping independent fields, not exclusions of this southern regional network. A different guitar model or album is not itself the category boundary. Authored energetic amplified-band rendition retains one accompanied singer, humbucker electric guitar with alder/maple construction and power-chord rhythm, dual-single-coil fingerstyle electric bass, and a maple stick-played kit with machine-hammered fully lathed traditional-finish cymbals. These materials and playing gestures are representative choices, not measured source equipment. The positively credited requinto uses the existing construction-unspecified owner, avoiding a false Veracruz/jarocho tradition or invented tuning and string count. Vocal register, physiology, pitch organization and ornament remain source-neutral; solo lead tracking is authored, not proof of absent original overdubs. Controlled live room is editorial, and optional capture apparatus/global tuning omitted. Whole-album union is not a claim the guest plays all twelve songs.","exemplars":["Sónica — Nada, complete twelve-track2002 album"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
